{{also|Abrahamman|Abraham-man}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|Abraham-man|Abrahamman|Abram man|Abram-man|Abramman}}

===Etymology===
From patients claiming, genuinely or not, to be temporarily discharged from the Abraham ward at Bethlem Royal Hospital (also known as Bedlam), a psychiatric hospital in London, for the purpose of begging. Possibly an allusion to a story in [[w:Gospel of Luke|Luke 16]]<!--(Murray (New Eng. Dict.))-->, in which the beggar Lazarus ends up in [[Abraham]]'s bosom. First use appears c. 1561 in ''The Fraternity of Vagabonds'' (1561) by {{w|John Awdely}}.

